Architecture

The application uses a layered, modular-monolith structure. Business rules are primarily handled in service modules rather than route handlers or Socket.IO event handlers.

HTTP Request
    |
    v
Routes
    |
    v
Middleware
(authentication, authorization, validation)
    |
    v
Controllers
    |
    v
Services
(business rules and transactions)
    |
    v
Sequelize Models
    |
    v
MySQL

Socket.IO is initialized on the same HTTP server as Express. Its responsibility is real-time authentication, private-room membership, and event delivery; ride acceptance remains controlled by the ride service and REST API.

Main API Areas

All REST endpoints are mounted under these prefixes:

Prefix

Purpose

/api/auth

Registration, login, token refresh, logout, profile, and password management

/api/rides

Ride requests, available rides, dispatch candidates, ride lookup, acceptance, start, completion, and cancellation

/api/cars

Driver vehicle creation and management

/api/drivers

Driver availability and location updates

/api/addresses

Saved-address management

/api/notifications

Notification listing, unread count, and read status

/api/admin

Administrative user blocking and unblocking

Most protected endpoints require a bearer access token. The exact request schemas and documented operations are available in Swagger UI.

Real-Time Events

Socket.IO clients authenticate by passing the access token in the handshake:

import { io } from "socket.io-client";

const socket = io("http://localhost:3000", {
  auth: {
    token: accessToken
  }
});

Event

Direction

Purpose

ride:offer

Server → eligible driver

Sends a ride offer to an authenticated driver's private room

ride:no-driver-found

Server → passenger

Reports that no eligible connected driver was found after the final dispatch stage

notification:new

Server → recipient

Delivers a persisted notification

Private room names are generated by the server from the authenticated user ID and role. Clients do not choose their own room identity.

Getting Started

Prerequisites

Node.js 18 or later.

MySQL server.

A Google Maps Routes API key for road-distance calculation when creating rides.

1. Get the source code

git clone <repository-url>
cd smart-taxi

2. Install dependencies

npm install

3. Configure environment variables

Create a local .env file from the example:

Windows PowerShell

Copy-Item .env.example .env

macOS / Linux

cp .env.example .env

Set the values in .env:

Variable

Description

PORT

HTTP server port (defaults to 3000)

NODE_ENV

Runtime environment; use development for local development

DB_HOST

MySQL host

DB_PORT

MySQL port

DB_NAME

Application database name

DB_USER

MySQL application username

DB_PASSWORD

MySQL application password

JWT_SECRET

Secret used to sign access tokens; use a long, unique secret

JWT_ACCESS_EXPIRES_IN

Access-token lifetime (defaults to 15m)

REFRESH_TOKEN_EXPIRES_DAYS

Refresh-token lifetime in days (defaults to 30)

GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY

Key used by the Google Routes API integration

FRONTEND_URL

Allowed frontend origin for CORS

Create the configured MySQL database and ensure the configured user has the required permissions before starting the application.

Do not commit .env or real credentials. The repository includes .env.example for variable names and placeholders.

4. Start the application

Development mode with automatic restart:

npm run dev

Regular start:

npm start

When the server is running, the main local URLs are:

API: http://localhost:3000

Health check: http://localhost:3000/health

Swagger UI: http://localhost:3000/api-docs

Change the port in .env if 3000 is already in use.

Testing

The project uses Node.js' built-in test runner.

Unit tests

npm test

The default test command runs the dispatch and notification unit-test files.

Integration infrastructure checks

npm run test:integration

This command runs database-safety, integration-infrastructure regression, and application-import checks.

Database-backed integration tests

The following commands exercise API behavior against the dedicated MySQL integration database. The current integration setup expects the database name smart_taxi_integration_local and the MySQL username smart_taxi_integration:

npm run test:integration:auth
npm run test:integration:car
npm run test:integration:ride
npm run test:integration:socket

The connection and schema setup checks are available separately:

npm run test:integration:connection
npm run test:integration:schema

Important: Database-backed integration tests require explicit integration-database configuration. Use only the dedicated disposable database smart_taxi_integration_local with the smart_taxi_integration user. The integration configuration also enforces a smart_taxi_integration_* name allowlist. Schema setup additionally requires explicit approval through SMART_TAXI_INTEGRATION_DB_APPROVED=true. Never point integration-test configuration at a development or production database. Review config/integrationDatabase.js and test/integration/ before running these commands.

Project Structure

smart-taxi/
├── app.js                    # HTTP server startup and graceful shutdown
├── appFactory.js             # Express application configuration
├── config/                   # Database, CORS, dispatch, Swagger, and migration setup
├── controllers/              # HTTP request/response handling
├── middleware/               # Authentication, authorization, and validation
├── migrations/               # Sequelize migration files
├── models/                   # Sequelize models and associations
├── routes/                   # REST API route definitions
├── services/                 # Business logic and transactional workflows
├── socket/                   # Socket.IO authentication, rooms, and event delivery
├── test/                     # Unit and integration tests
├── utils/                    # Shared utilities and application errors
├── .env.example              # Environment-variable template
└── package.json

Current Design Considerations

Ride-offer dispatch timers and attempt state are stored in process memory. They are cleared when a ride is accepted or cancelled and during application shutdown; they are not durable across process restarts. A multi-instance deployment would require shared coordination/state.

The application currently synchronizes Sequelize models during startup outside integration mode. Development mode also enables User.sync({ alter: true }). Although migration files and a Umzug configuration exist, migration execution is not currently wired into the application startup flow. Review schema-management behavior before using this project in production.

Google Routes API access is external and requires a valid API key and appropriate Google Cloud configuration.

